WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.160
Imagine a world where anything you can&nbsp;
think of can become a visual reality.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.040
A Watermelon Pie? We got it. A Cactus ghost?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.160
Got it. Hunter S Thompson as&nbsp;
the president? We got that too.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.920
For the last several days I’ve been obsessed with&nbsp;
an AI imagine generator called “Midjourney.” You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.160
put in a prompt like “the perfect cloud” and it&nbsp;
spits out some results. Sometime the results are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:32.880
awful, sometimes they’re bizarrely terrifying,&nbsp;
but just as many times they’re incredible.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.760
Here’s a movie camera by Pablo Piccaso.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.240
Infinite Cabbage. / A bathroom designed by&nbsp;
MC Escher. / A person made entirely out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.800
cheese. / Deluxe beans. / A strip mall&nbsp;
painted by monet. / Dune directed by Wes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.800
Anderson. / A bubble gum farm. / “Family Dinner”&nbsp;
by David Lynch / A Symbol for Bliss / Chicken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.360
Nuggets at a michelin star restaurant. /&nbsp;
Cotton Candy grapes. / Twin Peaks drawn by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.640
a child. / A potted tongue plant.
/ Pills by Gucci

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.760
I’ve spent hours surfing through what is&nbsp;
essentially humanity’s collective imagination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.160
remixed by my random ideas and a powerful&nbsp;
Artificial intelligence. It’s addicting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.320
and fascinating. Every idea is like a gamble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.880
you don’t know if it’s going to spit out&nbsp;
something amazing or just a pile of trash.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.840
sorry, a box of trash by Van Gogh

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.520
But it’s as terrifying as it is fascinating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:26.960
As this technology improves, (which it already&nbsp;
is; DALL-E 2 Google’s AI image generator is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:34.960
much more powerful than Midjourney) What will&nbsp;
it mean for art, images, imagination? What is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:46.240
a world where anything we imagine can become a&nbsp;
reality? What if we put everything on a bagel?

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:55.600
That Funny Feeling&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.560
There’s a new feeling I’ve been feeling&nbsp;
occasionally in recent years. It’s hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:16.880
to describe, deeply personal, overwhelming,&nbsp;
fractal, uncomfortable, and yet enticing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.040
awe-inspiring. For a while I wasn’t sure what&nbsp;
it was. I wasn’t sure where it was coming from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.760
or if anyone else was experiencing&nbsp;
what I was occasionally feeling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.887
And then- in 2020, I started noticing some&nbsp;
artists starting to depict the feeling.

00:02:29.887 --> 00:02:31.120
The first person who really put&nbsp;
a finger on a part of the feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.840
was Bo Burnham with his Comedy Special Inside:

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.360
That funny feeling.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.000
But another recent work- that captures&nbsp;
the feeling in an even more fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.440
way I think is the incredible, hypermodern,&nbsp;
maximalist film Everything Everywhere All At Once.&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.320
It’s easy to get bogged down in the technical&nbsp;
elements of “multiverses” and how they work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.520
either as a storytelling device, or as an actual&nbsp;
theory for our own cosmos. But what I’m most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:24.240
interested in about the multiverse, as it’s used&nbsp;
in Everything Everywhere all at once, is as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:31.840
metaphor for our own world and our own experience.&nbsp;
And for in a certain sense, the experience of:

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:37.120
Title: “The Internet”&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.360
I love the internet. For someone who’s always&nbsp;
loved learning about new and novel things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.225
who has a kind of insatiable curiosity, and could&nbsp;
spend all my time just endlessly exploring and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:49.600
learning about what’s out there if I didn’t have&nbsp;
to also do other things like socialize, pay rent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:54.960
and eat: the internet has been a garden of&nbsp;
endless wonder, delight and possibility.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.800
The internet has an inexhaustible variety&nbsp;
of cool stuff, tons of fascinating people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:06.800
and ideas to discover. The internet is why I’m&nbsp;
able to have such a cool and interesting job.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:19.120
But it’s also a double edged sword. Even when&nbsp;
it’s pointed towards what on the surface feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.360
like productive learning, and not just&nbsp;
mindless entertainment, when given the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:29.520
option to fulfill endless curiosity, there comes&nbsp;
a tipping point, a crossing of the bell curve.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:45.440
Here’s the thing: We don’t need an actual&nbsp;
multiverse to put cracks in the clay pot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:51.120
of our mind when we already have devices for&nbsp;
careening through the endless imaginations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.000
the multitudes, when we exist in an environment&nbsp;
where you can encounter the personal stories and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.640
experiences from people on every continent,&nbsp;
all who are living their own unique life in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:05.360
just a few minutes, all from the comfort&nbsp;
of your own toilet. When more interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:11.920
ideas and concepts, and people and places can fly&nbsp;
by in the space of one 30 minute Tik Tok binge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:17.200
then some of our ancestors experienced in the&nbsp;
entirety of their localized illiterate lives.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.960
The internet, for those who are inspired to spend&nbsp;
lot of time on it and use it in a certain way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:27.920
for those who envelope themselves in it’s&nbsp;
self-referential world of constantly evolving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:33.920
novelty and imagery, will inevitably have a&nbsp;
profound effect on the way you see the world.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.600
The Overview Effect
What is the result of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:39.040
the headlong dive through novel ideas and imagery?

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.720
Many Astronauts, upon seeing earth&nbsp;
for the first time from space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:49.920
spinning by below them report something&nbsp;
that has been dubbed “The Overview Effect”

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.840
“It is the experience of seeing&nbsp;
first-hand the reality of the Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.360
in space, which is immediately understood to be a&nbsp;
tiny, fragile ball of life, "hanging in the void",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.103
shielded and nourished by a paper-thin atmosphere.

00:06:01.103 --> 00:06:02.355
These astronauts are noting a&nbsp;
difficult-to-describe shift in their psyche&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.354 --> 00:06:07.360
as a result of their extreme shift in physical&nbsp;
perspective. And while I don’t think the effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:14.480
of being “very online” is same as The Overview&nbsp;
Effect that astronauts experience, I do think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:21.680
there’s an analogous effect that can happen from&nbsp;
consuming the amount of information many of us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:30.160
now do. Seeing so much of the world so quickly can&nbsp;
trigger a sort of psychological paradigm shift.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:35.920
The shift isn’t coming from being exposed to a new&nbsp;
idea of perspective that forces you to reassess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:42.960
your own, the shift comes from the shear amount&nbsp;
of perspectives and ideas that we’re exposed to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:07:03.547
The shift and the feeling of this shift can&nbsp;
contain some positives, but it isn’t all good.

00:07:03.547 --> 00:07:09.520
I think for most people the Digital Overview&nbsp;
Effect is a gradual and pervasive accumulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:15.600
slowly building up as we swim through a world&nbsp;
of thoughts, ideas, concepts and emotions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:21.440
all projected at us through images, text, and&nbsp;
video, one after another in an endless parade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:28.000
Each new idea can pull you in a different&nbsp;
direction, and there’s a certain point where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:36.080
everything, the infinite possibility starts to&nbsp;
look and feel like nothingness, at at that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:49.249
the entire thing collapses on itself. Sincerity&nbsp;
is long gone, but irony has also died.

00:07:49.249 --> 00:07:49.520
//

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.920
For me Everything Everywhere All at once&nbsp;
is one of the first true post-internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.920
films. It’s not just about the internet,&nbsp;
it doesn’t just comment one the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:04.320
but fully embraces it in all it’s absurdist,&nbsp;
chaotic glory. The maximalist excess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.680
and the complete tonal whiplash from one&nbsp;
moment to the next, captures the unhinged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:14.960
unfiltered anarchy of using the internet in a way&nbsp;
that nothing else I’ve ever consumed ever has.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:26.560
This is a movie for people who have found&nbsp;
themselves blinking sleep out of their eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:32.960
with 50 tabs open at 2am, which I suspect&nbsp;
is many more of us than we openly admit.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:38.240
In an interview with Slashfilm, Daniel&nbsp;
Scheinert, one of the movie’s directors said:

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.680
“We've talked a lot about what it's&nbsp;
like to have grown up with the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:46.720
and how that exacerbated the typical generational&nbsp;
divide, and what it feels like for everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:52.240
no matter old you are, to live right now with the&nbsp;
internet. So, that's one of the key metaphors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:56.960
was just like, we wanted the maximalism of&nbsp;
the movie to connect with what it's like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:01.680
scroll through an infinite amount of stuff,&nbsp;
which is something we're all doing too much.”

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:08.560
Most media that tries to comment on the internet,&nbsp;
comments on the “content” of the internet, or one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:15.040
small element that it wants to critique, but it&nbsp;
doesn’t comment on the message that comes from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:24.000
medium of the internet. The way the most absurd&nbsp;
or inane things you’ve ever seen coexist in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:31.040
immediate context of tragedy and your family and&nbsp;
friends. Everything Everywhere All At Once does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:37.920
It’s silly and stupid and choatic&nbsp;
because, well. Welcome to the Internet.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:49.440
Jobu Tupaki is a child of the internet,&nbsp;
raised in and by the chaos of the multiverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:54.160
cast unknowingly by her parents, without training&nbsp;
wheels, into the void. She doesn’t have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.160 --> 00:10:00.640
skills to cope, and neither do many of the real&nbsp;
teenagers who are growing up on the internet now.

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:06.880
Evelyn first tries to resist, and then&nbsp;
tries to understand, but realizes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:12.160
she too is not equipped to handle the&nbsp;
onslaught of experiencing the internet head on.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:16.720
How do we handle this situation?&nbsp;
How do we stare head on into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:23.280
void? Maybe you haven’t experience what I’m&nbsp;
talking about, maybe you don’t know what I mean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:29.360
Maybe you’ve been able to venture into this Brave&nbsp;
New World we’re living in relatively untouched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:38.560
but even if you haven’t I would argue that you&nbsp;
have. Our world is being shaped and formed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:44.400
people who have gone in and haven’t come&nbsp;
out unscathed, while my parents generation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:51.360
and even mine got to grow up with the internet as&nbsp;
something that you still went to as box to log on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:57.920
to, current generations are growing up with it as&nbsp;
an omnipresent extension of themselves. They grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:02.480
up in a technological world increasing dominated&nbsp;
by the internet, where it’s intertwined with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:11.280
education, social lives, and careers,&nbsp;
and where it increasingly takes a bold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:15.440
and defiant individual to take a step&nbsp;
away, much less disconnect entirely.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.440
And it’s not slowing down. While&nbsp;
the adults in power are just now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:24.880
becoming aware of the issues that began rearing&nbsp;
their heads in a decade ago, the next decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:30.880
holds even more astonishing wonders and terrors,&nbsp;
waiting for us to embrace them with open arms.

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:35.840
It might sound from all this that I&nbsp;
think it’s a very hopeless situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:38.880
But I don’t really think that, or at least I&nbsp;
don’t feel hopeless, and neither does Everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:45.360
Everywhere All at Once, which ultimately&nbsp;
affirms a quiet acceptance of a life that openly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:53.120
acknowledges the infinite chaos and possibility,&nbsp;
but quietly ignores it and makes the choice to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:58.480
value more immediate and personal things.&nbsp;
And in some sense while chaos reigns on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:02.000
planetary scale, it is possible I think for an&nbsp;
individual to find a better, healthier way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:02.771
engage with this Brave New World, and while that&nbsp;
doesn’t fix the problem as a whole, if we can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.771 --> 00:12:03.316
figure out how to engage with these things&nbsp;
in a healthy way as individuals, we’ll never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.316 --> 00:12:03.356
figure out how to do it as communities.

00:12:03.356 --> 00:12:09.440
And to engage with this world in a healthier way,&nbsp;
you’ll have to understand what doesn’t matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:24.320
Because the internet is a place&nbsp;
where everything matters all at once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:29.200
all the time. But the real word, the&nbsp;
life you’re living, is one where you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:37.840
only pay attention to a few thing in one&nbsp;
place, at a time. And giving your time and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:42.480
attention to a few things that do matter&nbsp;
means looking an almost infinite world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:47.760
possibility constantly vying for you attention&nbsp;
and boldly proclaiming that it doesn’t matter.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:06.640
There’s a quote from Marshall McLuhan that gives&nbsp;
me hope when thinking about these kinds of issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:08.960
so I’ll leave you with that:

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:14.720
“There is no inevitability, so long as there is&nbsp;
a willingness to contemplate what is happening.”

